WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.095
A trust boundary appears whenever a file moves from one context into another.

2
00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:10.661
Browser uploads and API uploads are obvious examples, but they are not the only ones.

3
00:00:10.661 --> 00:00:25.005
Imported spreadsheets, email attachments, shared folder drops, cloud storage objects, generated reports, temporary files, archives, third-party file feeds, and local administrator files all cross boundaries.

4
00:00:25.005 --> 00:00:30.884
The receiving system should decide what it is willing to accept before acting on the file.

5
00:00:30.884 --> 00:00:35.431
Internal files can still be malformed, stale, oversized, or unsafe.

6
00:00:35.431 --> 00:00:38.409
A partner feed may be generated by a buggy job.

7
00:00:38.409 --> 00:00:41.153
A shared folder may contain an old format.

8
00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:46.091
A support engineer may upload a troubleshooting file with sensitive data.

9
00:00:46.091 --> 00:00:52.049
A report generated by another service may include fields that changed after a deployment.

10
00:00:52.049 --> 00:01:00.044
Treating internal files as automatically trusted is how file-handling assumptions spread into production workflows.

11
00:01:00.044 --> 00:01:06.629
The file's origin should influence the control set, but it should not eliminate controls entirely.

12
00:01:06.629 --> 00:01:13.056
Public uploads may need strict size limits, authentication, malware scanning, and quarantine.

13
00:01:13.056 --> 00:01:18.700
Internal imports may need schema checks, version checks, and ownership validation.

14
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:26.382
Cloud object ingestion may need bucket policy review, object metadata validation, and event source verification.

15
00:01:26.382 --> 00:01:30.380
Different sources need different controls, not a free pass.

16
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.769
Good design names each file boundary and assigns responsibility.

17
00:01:34.769 --> 00:01:36.964
Who is allowed to submit the file.

18
00:01:36.964 --> 00:01:38.767
What formats are accepted.

19
00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.021
How large can it be.

20
00:01:40.021 --> 00:01:41.275
Where is it stored.

21
00:01:41.275 --> 00:01:42.608
Who can download it.

22
00:01:42.608 --> 00:01:44.254
Which parser handles it.

23
00:01:44.254 --> 00:01:46.684
What happens when validation fails.

24
00:01:46.684 --> 00:01:55.777
These questions should be answered before files reach business logic, storage, downstream processors, or user-facing download paths.
